          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "890bdffb96a26d674981622614d43b1f52f4864aa0a4b60b2af7d1450500cbb0",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-890bdffb96a2-0000-0006"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 3,
                "start": 3
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "The petitioners argue that \u201cnow\u201d must be given its ordinary, present\u2011time meaning, and that Congress intended the statute to apply to tribes that are presently under federal jurisdiction."
        }
      ],
      "heading": "Petitioners\u2019 Position",
      "status": "complete",
      "summary": null,
      "summary_citations": []
    },
    {
      "claims": [
        {
          "attribution": "MS. MAYNARD",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "890bdffb96a26d674981622614d43b1f52f4864aa0a4b60b2af7d1450500cbb0",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-890bdffb96a2-0001-0004"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 26,
                "start": 26
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to MS. MAYNARD, the respondents contend that the phrase \u201cnow under Federal jurisdiction\u201d was meant to refer to the status of tribes at the time the statute was enacted, not to a later, changing condition."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Petitioners",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "890bdffb96a26d674981622614d43b1f52f4864aa0a4b60b2af7d1450500cbb0",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-890bdffb96a2-0002-0004"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 56,
                "start": 55
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Petitioners, they also argue that the Secretary\u2019s authority to take property from a state would strip the state of jurisdiction and give it to a dual sovereign, illustrating the practical effect of the statutory language."
        }
      ],
